src/backend/executor/README.instrument

Instrumentation
===============

The instrumentation subsystem measures time, buffer usage and WAL activity
of query execution and similar work.  EXPLAIN ANALYZE, pg_stat_statements
and other consumers build on it.

It is designed so that:

* measuring a section of code is cheap, even when the section runs very
  often and only the aggregate matters, as with per-node instrumentation in
  the executor;

* nested measurements add up correctly, also when execution is aborted by
  an error.

The data structures are defined in src/include/executor/instrument.h, and
the implementation lives in src/backend/executor/instrument.c.


Entries
-------

An Instrumentation is the basic unit of measurement: it collects time,
buffer and WAL usage.  Specialized variants build on it:

QueryInstrumentation   A statement entry, for a query, a utility command or
                       a comparable unit of work.  It is the parent of the
                       child entries below, and takes care of abort cleanup.

NodeInstrumentation    A child entry with per-plan-node statistics (tuple
                       counts, loops, startup time, etc.).

Other code can register child entries with a statement entry as well, to
measure a part of its execution separately.


The Stack
---------

Buffer and WAL counters are not measured by taking a snapshot of global
counters and computing the difference afterwards: with a dozen or so
fields, doing that around every call of every plan node is too expensive.
Instead, there is a per-backend stack of entries, and the buffer manager,
WAL insertion etc. add their counts directly to the entry on top of it, the
current entry.  Entries start out zeroed, so each one holds exactly what
happened while it was on top.  Below the stack is a session-level entry,
which is current when nothing else is.

For example, while a query runs a Seq Scan below a NestLoop, the stack is:

    session entry          (implicit bottom)
    0: statement entry
    1: NestLoop
    2: Seq Scan            <-- current

Entries are pushed when they start and popped when they stop.  Only entries
that track buffer or WAL usage go on the stack; time is measured
independently of it.  Plain Instrumentation entries, used outside of statement
entries, still take snapshots of global counters instead.

Plan node counters are thus exclusive of the nodes below: they only count
what happened while the node itself was on top, which includes statements
it ran, e.g. through a function.  EXPLAIN computes the inclusive numbers it
shows by summing subtrees when it prints the plan.  Plan node counters are
never modified after the fact, which also keeps them comparable between the
leader and parallel workers.


Statement Totals
----------------

The total of a statement is its own counters plus those of all its child
entries.  It is computed whenever the statement entry is stopped; child
entries are never added to anything themselves.

A statement can be run in several steps: each FETCH from a cursor runs the
cursor's query a bit further, with other statements running in between.
The activity of each step belongs to what ran that step, not to the
statement that closes the cursor in the end.  So each time a statement
entry is stopped, it passes on what its total gained since the previous
stop to the entry that is current at that point, its parent.  When the
entry is finalized, it passes on whatever is left.

A statement run while another one executes, e.g. through SPI from a
function, is a nested statement.  If it has an entry, passing on its total
takes its activity to what ran it, typically a plan node of the enclosing
statement.  Statements that nobody asked to instrument have no entry: their
activity goes straight to the current entry while they run, with the same
end result.

Utility statements are measured by their consumer, e.g. pg_stat_statements,
with a statement entry that lives in the consumer's stack frame rather than
being tied to a resource owner, since the command may end the transaction
while running.  The consumer must finalize it from a PG_FINALLY block.


Abort Safety
------------

An error can abandon a statement at any point, leaving entries on the stack
that will never be stopped.  A statement entry is therefore registered with
the resource owner that is current when it first starts, and it stays
registered until it is finalized, in particular also between the steps of a
cursor.  On abort, the resource owner cleanup unwinds the stack to below the
entry, popping everything above it, and passes on what the entry's total
has not passed on yet.

That is exactly what a regular stop does, and passing on again without any
activity in between passes on nothing.  Hence the normal and the abort path
can never count anything twice, and the order in which the resource owner
releases entries does not matter.  Child entries need no cleanup of their
own, since they are only part of their statement's total.  The per-node
breakdown of an aborted statement is lost, but its activity reaches the
entry that was current when it started.


Memory
------

Entries must stay valid until their final pass-on, which on abort happens
after portal memory has already been released.  A statement entry that goes
on the stack therefore has a dedicated memory context, in which its child
entries must be allocated as well.  The context starts out below the
caller's memory, so that it goes away with it if the statement never runs,
moves under TopMemoryContext while a resource owner is responsible for it,
and goes back below the caller's memory once the entry is finalized.


Parallel Query
--------------

Each parallel worker measures its own buffer and WAL activity during its
part of the query, and reports the totals, which include what its plan nodes
did, to the leader when it is done.  The leader adds them to the totals of
the statement that launched the workers.  When per-node instrumentation is
active, a worker also reports its per-node statistics, which the leader keeps
apart from its own, and which EXPLAIN combines with them when it prints the
plan; they are for display only.


Rules
-----

* A child entry's counters must only change while its statement entry is
  running, i.e. between a start and the following stop.  Otherwise the
  activity is passed on to the wrong parent.

* Read the totals of a statement with InstrTotalUsage(), not from the
  statement entry's own counters, which do not include its children.

* Allocate child entries in their statement entry's memory context.
